<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stelvio Pass</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stilfser Joch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stelvio Pass is a mountain pass in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at 2,757 metres, one of the highest paved passes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valtellina">Valtellina</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinschgau">Vinschgau</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>. Until 1918 it lay on the border between Italy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and was fought over in 1866 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stelvio-pass.jpg" alt="Stelvio Pass" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kallerna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stelvio_Pass_Bolzano_side_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
